{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he shall lay his hand</b> — with both his hands, for we find \"And Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands upon the head of the bull\" (Exodus 29:10), \"And Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands upon the head of the ram\" (ibid. 15), and the Sages derived from this the hands of each and every individual, and it is with both his hands. And in the case of the scapegoat it is explicitly stated: \"And Aaron shall lay both his hands upon the head of the live goat\" (Leviticus 16:21). Now I do not know, if so, why Scripture writes in all the other semikhah passages \"his hand\" (אֶת יָדוֹ). Perhaps it is to exclude from it what the Sages derived (Menahot 93a): \"his hand\" — and not the hand of his agent, for perhaps had it said \"his hands\" it would have been understood to include both of them, and the agent would not be excluded; but now that the semikhah is with both his hands, Scripture did not write the singular form except to exclude the agent — for even though his agent is like him in other matters, this is not so ruled regarding semikhah. And in Torat Kohanim (after parashah 16:21): \"And Aaron shall lay both his hands\" (Leviticus 16:21) — this teaches that semikhah is with both hands, a general principle for all semikhah instances, that they must be with both hands." "<b>And it shall be accepted for him to make atonement for him</b> — for what does it make atonement for him? If you would say: for karet and for deaths by the court, or for death by the hand of Heaven, or for lashes — behold, their punishment is stated, and so it makes atonement only for a positive commandment and for a negative commandment that is converted into a positive commandment. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 1:4). And it is a baraita in Torat Kohanim (chapter 4:8). And I am astonished: for where is their punishment stated? For the offerings bring atonement in cases of unwitting transgression. We may say that they atone for those liable to death by the hand of Heaven unwittingly, and for those liable to lashes unwittingly, and for those liable to death by the court unwittingly — in those cases for which one is not obligated to bring a hataat, such as one who strikes his father or mother and one who curses [God], just as the hataat atones for those liable to karet unwittingly. Or perhaps it is because Scripture specified for those liable to death by the court and for those liable to karet their punishment both for intentional and unwitting transgression, and specified for those liable to death by the hand of Heaven their punishment for intentional transgression as death, and for those liable to negative commandments as lashes, but did not specify for them any punishment for unwitting transgression — it appeared to the Sages that all the punishment in them, Scripture has already specified. For why would He specify their punishment for these, both intentional and unwitting, and specify the punishment of the others for intentional transgression but not specify it for unwitting transgression and say that one is obligated to bring an olah for them? Therefore they saw that for those liable to death by the hand of Heaven and for those liable to lashes there is only the punishment explicitly stated for them for intentional transgression, but for unwitting transgression there is upon them no bearing of sin at all, and they require no atonement whatsoever. And this is what they said: \"their punishment is already stated\" — meaning that all the punishment He wished to impose upon them, Scripture has already stated. But for a positive commandment and for a negative commandment converted into a positive commandment, which are done intentionally and in which Scripture did not mention any punishment — and it is impossible that there be no punishment for them — by these He will be accepted through this olah if he brings it of his own voluntary offering.\n\nAnd it is also possible to say that because Scripture did not mention regarding the freewill offerings \"to make atonement for him for his unwitting sin which he committed,\" as it did regarding the hataat offerings, but said \"and it shall be accepted,\" our Sages inferred from this implication that it atones for intentional transgressions that are not acceptable before Him — for the unwitting transgressor, although he sinned, is acceptable to HaShem. If so, it is impossible for it to atone for intentional transgressions other than for a positive commandment and for a negative commandment converted into a positive commandment, in which no punishment is mentioned — except that they are not acceptable to the King because they transgressed His commandment, and by what shall they be reconciled to their Master? By this gift.\n\nAnd I saw in an aggadah in Vayikra Rabbah (7:3): Rabbi Shimon ben Yohai taught: the olah comes only for transgressions of the heart's thought. Rabbi Levi said: it is a full verse — \"And that which comes up upon your spirit — it shall not be\" (Ezekiel 20:32); the olah atones for that which comes up upon your spirit. And so in Job it says: \"And offer burnt-offerings according to their number, all of them, for Job said: Perhaps my sons have sinned and blessed Elohim in their hearts\" (Job 1:5) — this teaches that the olah comes only for the thoughts of the heart. And the reason is that since it is a sin that none recognizes but HaShem, therefore it is entirely consumed for HaShem.\n\nAnd the expression \"and it shall be accepted for him\" (וְנִרְצָה לוֹ) refers back to the honored Name mentioned above, that He will be reconciled to him through this offering to make atonement for him — in the sense of \"and with what shall this man be reconciled to his master\" (1 Samuel 29:4), \"for the light of Your countenance, for You were reconciled to them\" (Psalms 44:4), and many similar verses. And it is also possible that \"and it shall be accepted\" (וְנִרְצָה) is a designation for the sin: his sin becomes reconciled for him to atone for him — in the sense of \"for her iniquity is reconciled\" (כִּי נִרְצָה עֲוֹנָהּ) (Isaiah 40:2), \"until the land was reconciled to its sabbaths\" (עַד רָצְתָה הָאָרֶץ אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתֶיהָ) (2 Chronicles 36:21), \"and they shall reconcile their iniquity\" (וְהֵם יִרְצוּ אֶת עֲוֹנָם) (Leviticus 26:43) — an expression of completion. And it is possible that it is like the first explanation, as though the iniquity is reconciled before HaShem — His anger no longer burns against it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {1 "In the phrase from Ezekiel 20:32, Ramban reads עלה (olah, burnt offering) into עולה (that which rises/comes up), a pun central to the midrashic argument; the translation preserves this by rendering the plain sense of the verse while noting the midrashic reading in parentheses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:52.413345901Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}